The [GIA 1ct D-VS1 at $3,140](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29333987?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is the correct starting stone. VS1 is reliably eye-clean in an emerald cut step facet at 1ct. D color means no warmth in any lighting.

GIA certification means the grade is accurate.

The [GIA 1.06ct D-VVS2 at $3,971](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29652437?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is a genuine upgrade — the slightly larger face-up footprint at 1.06ct is visible in the ring, and VVS2 is exceptional eye-clean performance. The $831 premium over D-VS1 is reasonable for the extra face-up size.

The [GIA 1.02ct E-IF at $6,010](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/27239762?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) costs $2,870 more than the D-VS1 for an IF clarity grade. Internally Flawless is the best clarity grade available. Nobody can see the difference between IF and VS1 with the naked eye at 1ct.

That $2,870 difference is a certificate premium, not a visible stone premium.

