## The Baguette Bridge

The escalating baguette side stone is the most authentically Art Deco setting choice for an emerald cut diamond ring. This is The Baguette Bridge: baguette side stones extend the rectangular geometry of the center stone outward along the finger. The two shapes share the same straight-edge language.

They are made for each other.

[![Vintage emerald cut diamond ring with baguette side stones — geometric Art Deco alignment](/images/504770_M1_EMR_DIM_wht_0100CT_W_Box5_001_1600X1600.avif)](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings?styleIDs=vintage&ShapeID=Emerald&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)

Round side stones frame a rectangular center stone. Marquise side stones compete with it — the pointed oval echoes different geometry than the flat step-cut table. Baguettes continue it.

The finger sees one unbroken horizontal line: baguette, center stone, baguette. That is how Art Deco rings from the 1920s and 1930s were designed. Not as a center stone with accessories.

As one continuous geometric statement.

